    "q-82293": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82293",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/cd6de23e9b33d0e86a91eb4bf6b81a9d443c1b389182f1c05bf10d048f0c0402.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f$ is a surjection\n\nII. $f$ is an injection\n\nIII. $f$ is a bijection",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider $y=5.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $5\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false,  $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
