    "q-82308": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82308",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/3e698d422a28d15fc4fa34776578b7e7507c627db6abeb44d18ac59b3f421065.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{e}^{x}-1$, which of the following statements are true?   \n\nI. The function is an injection\n\nII. The function is a surjection\n\nIII. The function is a bijection",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/fb4b6291f9897a57dd2980982fecba8930ea9406ecc46a88e547b62d36a20ab7.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is not $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=3.$ So, $f$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false, because the function is not  surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only. \"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
