    "q-76261": {
      "dom_id": "step-q76261",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/83fc79d2043c3a4783f394938e613fea701ae04bcf97d4e13f4f5ab11e1d329a.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2{x}^{3}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The function is an injection\n\nII. The function is a surjection\n\nIII. The function is a bijection",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/5b187aa10f1a28b81d06f47383b8fec0415607fac043c5bf38665aeb07cbac16.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
