      "dom_id": "step-q82571",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/035791141568cecc50f5e10a94decca871af66c9bc49aa70e02c7a4714b9dbce.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to (-\\infty ,2]$ defined by $f(x)=2-{e}^{-x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The function is an injection\n\nII. The function is a surjection\n\nIII. The function is a bijection",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/8ed6866a4ea7d2e1b2bb9c9ea8d7466935c8cda54e8ef3bf70c5947f6a65be0e.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is false. There is no value $x$ for which $f(x)=2\\in (-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
