    "q-82573": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82573",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/baef3d37780e1bb2fbdbac7fa1bc0aa00961cfc5b70b51c27b775fa4454ffcaf.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to (-1,1]$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}+1}-1$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The function is an injection\n\nII. The function is a surjection\n\nIII. The function is a bijection",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $∃\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/01b104fe49519b2de16810e7f8e489c3e19a0d6d8dd9d2ace13e226d4aebd35c.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $(-1,1].$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
