    "q-82570": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82570",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/93ea784f1866e884f79627ae9e84c4169e2f7aee74c6a235c35de4ee7531834a.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The function is an injection\n\nII. The function is a surjection\n\nIII. The function is a bijection",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14256507/assets/a3c8e45c03b1d0533adfff3353691644c398a18b893b2370dd14f6dce1b27441.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
