    "q-77821": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77821",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=|x|$\n\nII. $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)=4(x-2)$\n\nIII. $h:Q\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. For example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 4(x-2) & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{4}+2\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{4}+2)=4([\\frac{y}{4}+2]-2)=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 4({x}_{1}-2) & =4({x}_{2}-2) \\\\ {x}_{1}-2 & ={x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
