    "q-76258": {
      "dom_id": "step-q76258",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}-1$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =y \\\\ {2}^{x} & =y \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(y) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that:\n\n$$\nf(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡(y))={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)}=y\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $f$ is both surjective and injective, $f$ is bijective.\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=2.$\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $h(x)={x}^{2}-1$ has two roots, $x=-1,1.$ So, we have $h(-1)=0=h(1),$ while $-1\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
